# Copy to .env.local and fill in. Also set these in your Vercel project
# (Project → Settings → Environment Variables). Everything NEXT_PUBLIC_ is
# public by design — it ships to the browser — so keep the Supabase
# service_role key out of here entirely.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-public-key

# --- No operator key here ----------------------------------------------------
# Writing sales into the payout vault used to be signed by a key in this file.
# It isn't any more: the contract names one address as its operator, and an
# operator signs credits in their own wallet from /admin, the same way a
# contributor signs a claim. This server holds no Stellar key that can touch
# money — nothing to leak, and nothing to set up here.
#
# ADMIN_WALLETS below decides who gets into /admin. It does not decide who the
# contract takes a credit from — that list lives on-chain, holds up to ten
# wallets, and is managed from the vault card by whoever holds the contract's
# admin key. The CLI equivalent is
#   stellar contract invoke --id <payout-contract> --source <admin-key> \
#     --network testnet -- add_operator --operator <wallet-address>
#
# Each wallet on that list needs a few test XLM of its own, for fees.

# --- Wallet sign-in ----------------------------------------------------------
# A wallet proves itself by signing a SEP-10 challenge. Two server-side secrets
# make that work, and neither has a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix for a reason.
#
# The key that signs challenges. It is never funded and never holds anything —
# its only job is to let step two recognise a challenge we issued. Generate one
# with: stellar keys generate datavar-auth && stellar keys show datavar-auth
STELLAR_AUTH_SECRET=SXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
#
# Supabase's own symmetric JWT secret, so the session tokens we mint are ones
# Supabase accepts — that is what lets row-level security read the wallet
# address out of the token instead of trusting the app to filter.
#
# Dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys → JWT Keys, where it is called
# "Legacy JWT Secret" (older projects: Settings → API → JWT Settings → JWT
# Secret). It is the same secret that signs the anon key above, which is how
# you can tell it is the right one: decode the anon key and its header says
# HS256.
#
# If this project is ever migrated to asymmetric signing keys AND the legacy
# secret is revoked, minting stops working — Supabase does not hand out the
# private half of an asymmetric key. The path then is to publish a JWKS
# endpoint and register it under Third-Party Auth, and have src/lib/auth/jwt.ts
# sign with our own key instead.
#
# Treat it like a password: anyone holding it can mint a session for any wallet.
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=your-supabase-legacy-jwt-secret

# --- Admin panel -------------------------------------------------------------
# Wallets allowed into /admin, comma-separated. Server-side: the browser is
# told whether it is an operator, inside a signed token, and never gets to
# decide. Row-level security checks the same claim again on every query.
ADMIN_WALLETS=GXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX

# --- Consent receipts (Soroban) ----------------------------------------------
# The deployed consent contract. Public by design: verifying a receipt is meant
# to need nobody's permission, so the address ships to the browser and anyone
# can call is_valid on it themselves. Source in contracts/contracts/consent.
#
# Deploy your own with:
#   cd contracts && stellar contract build
#   stellar contract deploy --wasm target/wasm32v1-none/release/consent.wasm \
#     --source <your-key> --network testnet -- --admin <your-address>
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONSENT_CONTRACT_ID=CXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX

# --- Payout vault (Soroban) --------------------------------------------------
# Where contributor earnings sit between the sale and the claim. The operator
# key can only credit against it; moving money out to a contributor takes that
# contributor's own signature. Public for the same reason as the consent
# contract: what it holds and what it owes should be checkable by anyone.
# Source in contracts/contracts/payout.
#
# Deploy your own with:
#   cd contracts && stellar contract build
#   stellar contract deploy --wasm target/wasm32v1-none/release/payout.wasm \
#     --source <your-key> --network testnet -- \
#     --admin <your-address> --operator <operator-address> \
#     --token $(stellar contract id asset --asset native --network testnet)
#
# Then put test XLM in. This is done from outside the app on purpose — the
# server has no key that can move funds, so there is no button for it:
#   stellar contract invoke --id <contract> --source <any-funded-key> \
#     --network testnet -- fund --from <that-key's-address> --amount 5000000000
NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYOUT_CONTRACT_ID=CXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX
